<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarvastivada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarvastivada was one of the early schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, named after its doctrine that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharmas</a>, the elements of existence, exist in past, present and future. Strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from about the 2nd century BCE, it produced a large Abhidharma literature, including the Mahavibhasa, and its monastic code was followed in oasis states such as Kucha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a>. Its ideas shaped later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarvastivada.jpg" alt="Sarvastivada" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marek Gawęcki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tapa_Shotor_seated_Buddha_(Niche_V1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
